[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ae2dc0-2c48-57fe-b77f-9f67bdd2e46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354715} {:answer/id #uuid "2e42ae41-89c0-5b47-bbb6-fb998225a484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355061} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3683ca-db72-5783-ae9e-4b338e571426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355337} {:answer/id #uuid "7392534c-9571-5b57-9ed2-8040fdba11dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355683} {:answer/id #uuid "8c59e2a9-7838-53b2-bb73-74bd5d9dd03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b3683ca-db72-5783-ae9e-4b338e571426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355337} :answer-field/id #uuid "224c6656-d13d-552e-80a0-a1cdf090e6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da6f4133-24dc-5140-aaa0-0ea075dfe3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-9255" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(14,6),$ and $(9,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007f0237-ad10-55bc-96ed-537c0b924d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354660} {:answer/id #uuid "11fcff98-4636-54be-aca6-915931063b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354827} {:answer/id #uuid "14c32ab5-b09a-5fdd-82ca-b53500065288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354845} {:answer/id #uuid "795d0faa-7625-51e4-bb91-86f69d1df25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355725} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab29af-ae5a-59e7-a74f-d9e3ee2a1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "795d0faa-7625-51e4-bb91-86f69d1df25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355725} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61999bc-41a8-5be5-8d80-0cb905616eba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1174578d-6631-5fcf-81bf-729c1fac3da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-9258" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(4,-1),(17,-3),$ and $(10,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112c3c85-bec9-56e4-a9ee-09149d0d3fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354821} {:answer/id #uuid "1d675587-ad44-5c63-aa9d-fd8d11f41890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354918} {:answer/id #uuid "31a76e84-b983-5def-ad11-edb615520444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355099} {:answer/id #uuid "94022cd9-9367-58e6-8da4-3b37e31b023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355969} {:answer/id #uuid "de106381-5201-5a91-8220-244c9c95b9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d675587-ad44-5c63-aa9d-fd8d11f41890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354918} :answer-field/id #uuid "5256f032-d750-537f-8b01-5c6ba0859d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f585952-0c84-59bf-89fe-d6813830d204" :question/math-academy-id "q-9260" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-21,0),(21,0),$ and $(0,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,-3)$\n- $(3,1)$\n- $(-3,3)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-7-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-3,0),(3,0),(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0430e1ba-777f-5b9f-a671-927dfc1b7336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354690} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2cf3b6-602b-5cf3-a7f8-c3947a72b871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355336} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb82dbf-e096-5819-a946-dff85100e7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356046} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6826c9-c659-5821-8dd5-1e16666c7bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356438} {:answer/id #uuid "f70ee03f-ba96-5e14-8ab8-61d5ee69963c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0430e1ba-777f-5b9f-a671-927dfc1b7336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354690} :answer-field/id #uuid "33db2135-ab30-5b0e-910b-265e95142ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a755a352-feaa-5539-8e01-5e0ad683e5ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-9261" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,2),(-5,4),(-3,2),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3-2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0),(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1764762c-de15-580d-a3dc-d3428413e244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354874} {:answer/id #uuid "22932542-91c1-5542-b992-39df3e777a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354956} {:answer/id #uuid "d5368fdb-4e16-5d03-a93d-b845ab3a5e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356529} {:answer/id #uuid "d60c7df3-68ec-5105-8af1-7b52c6ddc66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356539} {:answer/id #uuid "f142f0c1-47d7-5f91-bfd0-c8a4dd1bd3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5368fdb-4e16-5d03-a93d-b845ab3a5e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356529} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d071199-1bd1-5314-aa05-d2b5389ab6b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c32a27cb-e2ec-5eaa-a1cb-1ea5655b2b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-9262" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217a9fbf-12ec-585e-8124-936aef700082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354948} {:answer/id #uuid "6211568c-3621-5b6b-b920-7eea97b2bfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355537} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad89b6f-5afc-54c9-ac6e-63308c1675f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356040} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b17d8e-dca4-5f59-a384-e7b13e8a71c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356125} {:answer/id #uuid "e1efb66b-43bd-55b1-b422-8000bbb8b8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad89b6f-5afc-54c9-ac6e-63308c1675f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356040} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7510a5-9947-5806-929b-8b2b4d20ca58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a6a4708-c258-5b5f-8c4c-fa774844e5ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-9263" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4c6020-b712-524b-9dd4-fd76dcbfa2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354768} {:answer/id #uuid "3238cf73-aac5-5d48-a4bf-5a341a260092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1e410-6a03-537a-a5e6-27c78ec91c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356228} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7306bd-678c-5c17-87a4-3a1c20658b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356339} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8193d6-5731-53cd-b9e3-025378afb6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4c6020-b712-524b-9dd4-fd76dcbfa2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354768} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4d1570-a4c9-589c-9f25-2961695bfcd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56746454-856c-5001-bcf8-93772c0debce" :question/math-academy-id "q-9264" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,0),(-2,-2),(-4,0),$ and $(-2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(-2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dded7e-582e-5ec1-b987-ce936b8780b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354719} {:answer/id #uuid "109b309a-f087-5d7e-b2c9-056fb6f7ebfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354813} {:answer/id #uuid "33a58d4a-c0e7-5328-895a-91ecd0236e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355118} {:answer/id #uuid "be29496e-4ea5-5052-a05a-c4eb5f330612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356329} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c070e4-1e49-54fc-8ce2-efbc05e5f661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be29496e-4ea5-5052-a05a-c4eb5f330612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356329} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc13cff-2d66-5429-a5b3-63a4f9a7d12d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95ad491b-4ee0-5e7b-8a0c-33d2b7cf1e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93715" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{′}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391ab15a-7637-50ca-8ad9-0d06dbc7db48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355177} {:answer/id #uuid "72e6cafb-5568-5033-8094-3bf866d01d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355675} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4f30a5-521c-543d-a973-483997a9b515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355930} {:answer/id #uuid "dae9edb1-a5bb-5c1f-81bc-26e592fe7d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356590} {:answer/id #uuid "f619a56c-197a-5bb8-8619-ce0c36b06cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae9edb1-a5bb-5c1f-81bc-26e592fe7d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356590} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e5c6e2-b88c-50be-9533-78e52f70d7c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6e8b60c-c6c9-5753-b254-94e3e9635292" :question/math-academy-id "q-93721" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aed7340-5f0e-5165-ab13-314353711b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355333} {:answer/id #uuid "8963ef11-348f-5e5f-8b9b-9f406cee908f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355880} {:answer/id #uuid "97fba95d-9f14-57b0-a978-2376e948fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356004} {:answer/id #uuid "bf821222-3076-59f2-8a78-9a7dfb62f4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356340} {:answer/id #uuid "e046d0c3-8ff2-5bd0-8adf-c6c8f1901ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8963ef11-348f-5e5f-8b9b-9f406cee908f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67b1e1e-56ae-5451-a8ba-26dd0254b3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5a30c2-2f06-5753-8caf-ff9645089df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354797} {:answer/id #uuid "47b9ccab-3108-5c8f-9b8c-5464d8508940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355301} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf821ab-99cc-5436-8de5-50227fd28ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355350} {:answer/id #uuid "543cd51b-5184-59d0-ac0b-707a18d83b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355413} {:answer/id #uuid "7c862cd4-aca7-5b9a-8fc3-e8126c91b2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf821ab-99cc-5436-8de5-50227fd28ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355350} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9482b40-d3eb-5583-9a45-f6fae5b035a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc49e39d-dc41-5d92-84ab-b1310e571077" :question/math-academy-id "q-93988" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8a088d-38ed-529a-a0ad-6f8904a93baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354791} {:answer/id #uuid "1171e5a0-776e-5f64-b0f4-c6ac65970f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354823} {:answer/id #uuid "375c1745-5919-5823-b480-6bcd0cf9f315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355162} {:answer/id #uuid "9c727cd7-331c-5b79-a010-330c635aef7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356055} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e19068-c9c1-5a4a-b765-7eb8327e35ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e19068-c9c1-5a4a-b765-7eb8327e35ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356780} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a19c8ce-0f70-556e-8f7f-0eec3b04e312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a5f4b21-f8de-5f12-8b8c-c4be1021742e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93993" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0210fb-2ce4-56bc-bc73-b65b45ac4543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354784} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd4b742-7213-5157-88b6-90e4d7ada2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355035} {:answer/id #uuid "38d118a2-4a5c-55de-9486-508ecb2e6afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355176} {:answer/id #uuid "3af32008-d6c8-59a5-b2a3-142c51facb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355196} {:answer/id #uuid "dd487c75-7ae4-5e3e-90b4-b5388c600452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bd4b742-7213-5157-88b6-90e4d7ada2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355035} :answer-field/id #uuid "712457e1-94be-50af-bf15-c91a42f71412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df2ac6ff-519f-58d2-90f1-c2ccb3e51740" :question/math-academy-id "q-94059" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $K$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $L$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since ${m}_{12}\\ne {m}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$K$ and $L$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e99577a-0495-5805-ae75-73567c392385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354801} {:answer/id #uuid "23050ebe-fa9b-5c46-b08c-336621c0e9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354962} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca267f3-0039-5f1e-be2b-16684827ea91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356058} {:answer/id #uuid "cffdd6fa-19d0-5433-a1c9-8294d86f15dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356481} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f01e3a-cc98-509d-9a98-0acef54b587b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca267f3-0039-5f1e-be2b-16684827ea91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356058} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1032e9-c794-58c0-8cda-b33f0b56e724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93d6f7f1-c1fb-5ae2-b8f7-98ecf5b306a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94062" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $U$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $V$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ and $U$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045899f4-0265-529a-b1bf-c97ae74f8f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354691} {:answer/id #uuid "06f47c75-3a6b-5eda-9070-00ecbce1a6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ and $F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354721} {:answer/id #uuid "1b85b833-ebd2-5180-863b-80c4f7aef2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354904} {:answer/id #uuid "1e09da54-089e-5bf2-9653-6d2d5423272a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354921} {:answer/id #uuid "973c589f-7e06-56a1-980d-1a5836e3bdc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "045899f4-0265-529a-b1bf-c97ae74f8f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354691} :answer-field/id #uuid "856cee2b-6bfa-5802-a288-b8a30743e419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61630213-687e-509d-b369-4384ce08371c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94063" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ has an associated $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $D$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}.$ Although it's a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $D$ is not symmetric since ${d}_{24}\\ne {d}_{42}.$\n\n- $E$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $4\\times 4$).\n\n- $F$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it is a $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$F$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040b3a63-19be-5022-9658-3014fb50b885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354689} {:answer/id #uuid "84455952-9fa7-5e94-aa0f-d7d5bfb8e11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355830} {:answer/id #uuid "b15c462d-1b5e-57a3-adf5-fc61bd9e16cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356226} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5fd280-3e1b-5029-8757-e4ee17ca35c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356711} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4c80ad-01b9-599e-8626-a6256b045b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15c462d-1b5e-57a3-adf5-fc61bd9e16cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356226} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbf2f3b-088a-5dda-9944-62e1f201bf5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b779ea2-64c9-5921-9b3e-0c6d8313380f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94275" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ebe2c9-20b0-526d-b03f-886e5998a3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354672} {:answer/id #uuid "30dc7bcb-7a0f-537f-ba30-09d2e3c4f233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355090} {:answer/id #uuid "373749a5-0a5c-571a-bbea-0dafb35136b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355160} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ca1f7-7c2d-50cc-931b-dcc6b7af5b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355619} {:answer/id #uuid "8be4f329-9b59-53d8-9b18-b76bd6467a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373749a5-0a5c-571a-bbea-0dafb35136b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355160} :answer-field/id #uuid "303256b6-8ac9-531c-a421-78bede8dc5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "784be4fd-7d61-5b65-af33-15597c14ae36" :question/math-academy-id "q-94276" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡16t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡16t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II could be used to represent $y(t).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6b5f63-aaf9-5bf3-95e6-9b8950c0ebf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355030} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6e07e2-1fd4-5462-93f1-8c5de91cae56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355043} {:answer/id #uuid "3e42fc49-b62b-5190-987e-6600bd37b53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355222} {:answer/id #uuid "46a572ac-6f34-573c-8869-c7d78b9613bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355289} {:answer/id #uuid "f0132a2c-0556-5640-9497-faf83c24096e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a572ac-6f34-573c-8869-c7d78b9613bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355289} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd674765-5c42-5338-b573-bc8ba66d6d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21aff269-b5d0-5ff1-9c3e-386be14a7e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-94287" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17920bc3-3b7e-5099-87bc-91f0890de4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354876} {:answer/id #uuid "50fc65d4-669e-5ee6-ab6a-9a016a3d0703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355386} {:answer/id #uuid "718cc4af-ab18-5c33-91dc-2366149f11ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355666} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e67558-ffcf-50f5-b7ad-431e6880fea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356414} {:answer/id #uuid "d04b8244-2acd-5697-ae90-8069de899546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "718cc4af-ab18-5c33-91dc-2366149f11ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355666} :answer-field/id #uuid "74851fbd-2c2f-57a2-86d5-0a1706c0c9ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de4a4e60-2947-5dae-80ca-6bd02414f29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94288" :question/problem "A particle of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=21\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+3y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 2 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2967b32a-c63e-5c49-9be1-0b312bf4e110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355009} {:answer/id #uuid "9cabe3c5-61f9-5a37-b58a-930b3dcbddd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356059} {:answer/id #uuid "a180d161-9846-5915-acd2-1e72bb38f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356099} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1bd449-ec1d-562c-b250-d8aedf3d49f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356459} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dc1545-d354-5c14-be22-e75d302df324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2967b32a-c63e-5c49-9be1-0b312bf4e110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355009} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a73dfb-d874-53f6-bddb-ac500704fb9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e4b9d3-5113-5f39-9a2d-eee9d1949dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94310" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29809ee8-292b-5c9c-913b-0bb723fc7ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355010} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac74971-63d8-5b23-8fcf-97c3ece1fcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355194} {:answer/id #uuid "3c628be3-3714-5241-9233-0637686d6ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355205} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ddee6f-5ab0-5a93-9234-09c063951125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356167} {:answer/id #uuid "b501d685-a302-5692-a5b9-3dc90442ee5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c628be3-3714-5241-9233-0637686d6ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355205} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd55c365-f91e-5a88-be8e-7db91b2b6861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6276abb-11ab-5f2c-8db5-0bce52daa784" :question/math-academy-id "q-94311" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a71eb77-c12c-5cec-bf11-0e5015bd9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355330} {:answer/id #uuid "58d37840-4038-5001-bec7-7d59f6b97472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355452} {:answer/id #uuid "72b3d522-e125-5325-b715-b212bf875da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355674} {:answer/id #uuid "76b2efa3-bff5-54ff-b62d-1e854ea6e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} {:answer/id #uuid "f08bc086-5017-5952-b23a-dc70bf9be510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b2efa3-bff5-54ff-b62d-1e854ea6e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df2e1f8-1c96-5da0-8938-5d076e74e024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2840cb37-803e-5355-a4ea-a188990899c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94313" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-4(1)(2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =13>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +14=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(14) & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1506779c-da1e-5258-a746-aa1010e48366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354853} {:answer/id #uuid "3415739f-1d61-518e-b0c7-ca0711994b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355124} {:answer/id #uuid "51a36235-9f5c-5e34-8851-c810469b4f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355394} {:answer/id #uuid "8512d7aa-7410-539e-a90f-0f1559ad9bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355840} {:answer/id #uuid "e91b7db7-efed-5209-b616-1a48649ae3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1506779c-da1e-5258-a746-aa1010e48366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354853} :answer-field/id #uuid "329eea82-d594-58e7-969e-bc96d3f41554" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feaf4026-120d-5dc5-a147-ce0975029f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-94376" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1bc7b6-4de7-5eac-8417-854e3e18c590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354757} {:answer/id #uuid "7da5b856-80e8-5cfd-bd82-d217ab37885e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355774} {:answer/id #uuid "8626180b-9f54-5542-bbda-3b519ffbbcb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355847} {:answer/id #uuid "a00f8753-5db0-5f2a-a18d-d3faa6c8c488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356085} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d3dc9e-cb4f-5ec5-920b-00a0a9195ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1bc7b6-4de7-5eac-8417-854e3e18c590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee1fe3e-224e-55a4-98f8-d5e86ba6333e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91ea3a27-d7dd-5526-bebd-b64e4b8069b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94377" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2439d162-c1a7-50dd-be17-052a6b25c8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354968} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4068fd-c326-57d2-be53-fa7da7f90186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355373} {:answer/id #uuid "59449265-6d91-52d5-85e9-4e540a910284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355455} {:answer/id #uuid "b7be5d4f-138b-5028-a41f-bbe4b335f3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356274} {:answer/id #uuid "e74b95f2-282e-591b-b3b7-d3a72e5d6d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7be5d4f-138b-5028-a41f-bbe4b335f3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356274} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b9a391-12d7-53d2-94ba-b63d5ec5be7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f4ed1b6-671a-54be-9f7b-f836039c55ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-94380" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-5,0]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-5,0]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[-5,0]\\,|\\,f(0)=0}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under function addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of $C[-5,0]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-5,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is  true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =kf(0) \\\\  & =k\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3922d0-9e98-50fc-badf-c5493ce5c08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354795} {:answer/id #uuid "66a11be1-b8f6-5bf3-865a-32797a40b01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355576} {:answer/id #uuid "81aec1db-2243-5668-a3b3-f7f2049fd7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355803} {:answer/id #uuid "9e22c7d3-b846-5397-9fb1-05bb1733229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356074} {:answer/id #uuid "b95128ac-6579-590f-8e02-4fa489a727c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95128ac-6579-590f-8e02-4fa489a727c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b4326e-a9f7-5be8-a7ee-fabfc0ce13c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbe86c7f-4360-5aea-8ad0-c457c70df4e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94382" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28786836-a9e1-53dc-9562-2281024f6d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355004} {:answer/id #uuid "8df5d5a1-0e16-5f9e-8fc4-5a02550067f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355917} {:answer/id #uuid "e9aca9c0-5eb9-57cf-8642-0bbd549233b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356708} {:answer/id #uuid "edfad9d8-8a31-5267-8053-334b53172752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356738} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6aef9c-ee3a-5af8-9168-9d60a704c091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edfad9d8-8a31-5267-8053-334b53172752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356738} :answer-field/id #uuid "851183af-a230-57df-8945-3d3a755a2ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf314c8-ddb9-53cd-832f-d87f0d4562fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-94383" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=10{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$\n- $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+10{e}^{t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053fe67-e452-5e43-9bb8-ae33f4d3e778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357883} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb575b8-c82d-587a-b04c-5707c757472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358294} {:answer/id #uuid "a147d160-a156-5831-8cba-5c29b4aca2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359156} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f42a5-a427-5e69-ad2f-206dbea65336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359187} {:answer/id #uuid "fa494a1c-d0a0-58f7-a9fb-58f8e537f805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb575b8-c82d-587a-b04c-5707c757472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358294} :answer-field/id #uuid "92270955-5282-5fef-9bf9-91d8246b119c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef92c245-5042-5dba-9942-02e46fcd2132" :question/math-academy-id "q-94612" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{2}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=2}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =2+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=2$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2\\cdot 2=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2b426f-f8af-5070-9e50-5a6b0a819c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357983} {:answer/id #uuid "36a0db23-ac83-59bc-973b-81496b995aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358235} {:answer/id #uuid "4c82fb7b-68a9-542f-8a72-aecf39118e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358415} {:answer/id #uuid "78d426a2-5b18-5538-be7f-d8d7711f2516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358797} {:answer/id #uuid "df6d19fb-dc41-5060-a6e5-ee17c97babb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c82fb7b-68a9-542f-8a72-aecf39118e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358415} :answer-field/id #uuid "9653397f-cce0-591e-9443-3d5aacbed087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec5fca83-ae3c-5f26-9292-3c61fcf8876c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94615" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{4}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $4,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{4}[t]\\,|\\,p(0)=1}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(0)=g(0)=1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)+g(0) & =1+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(0)=1$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(0) & =1\\cdot 2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cba9378-aa65-5f79-bbb4-49f2945dbb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358561} {:answer/id #uuid "79459e7d-932c-570a-876c-2070e8118132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358804} {:answer/id #uuid "7f073c7f-ec1d-5a96-b123-4770e6d5cc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358865} {:answer/id #uuid "c48e9462-f861-5de3-9393-b978b3c5cb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359478} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ad2bca-2d39-5290-b741-36cf455d63d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48e9462-f861-5de3-9393-b978b3c5cb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359478} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc24de32-fc55-5883-8618-dfe761bb7c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ffc0d2e-16d3-51e9-8958-0f5807298d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-94624" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $E$ containing all diagonal $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $E$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $E$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $E$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $E,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $E,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e76ee2-c4de-511f-bb83-b89976ff2b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358260} {:answer/id #uuid "7e89e201-8be2-57cf-989e-8e6190145401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358855} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe29dc3-575f-5623-bedd-cbfae69fc15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359543} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ef9bcb-2d57-5322-ba3c-91772e3cb4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359897} {:answer/id #uuid "f97547f0-48b9-53f5-b05f-2a6934a40ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbe29dc3-575f-5623-bedd-cbfae69fc15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359543} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a0e4b0-8f3a-5a00-943a-9bf5b93b46a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32515201-cdee-5802-875e-aaeaf04119b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94625" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ 1-a & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445107e9-f64f-5656-a6d0-d9884661cd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358337} {:answer/id #uuid "45972b24-c37c-50ab-9a82-558ffd8d06ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358345} {:answer/id #uuid "4704fed8-71ee-52ff-af45-fe415ea1fadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358365} {:answer/id #uuid "5e314ee9-7935-5c2b-99df-8d43abd5bdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358576} {:answer/id #uuid "acafb6e1-a2aa-501d-b2fa-0009a9fb5806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "445107e9-f64f-5656-a6d0-d9884661cd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358337} :answer-field/id #uuid "5138f6f1-ce72-5014-b6b7-6168114c4ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c523e99-06d7-537c-99b6-2e21ff3e81b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94629" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $F={A\\in {\\text{M}}_{2}(R)\\,|\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A=A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $F$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $F$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $F,$ then ${A}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{2}.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2})[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & ={A}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{A}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{1}+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]({A}_{1}+{A}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $F,$ then $A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A.$ For $k\\in R,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kA)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & =k\\cdot A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}](kA).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $kA$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $F$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a3bcb9-87cf-5e9a-a453-b12b6d936c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357759} {:answer/id #uuid "44258477-180c-53ea-9ca7-2338a1643dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358334} {:answer/id #uuid "56da8d21-6470-58b6-8612-c05ed3e6c73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358506} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7caca1-2a0b-555a-94b6-8df04eac42e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359134} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c9dd1d-a17a-54df-a66c-3ac461bac62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c9dd1d-a17a-54df-a66c-3ac461bac62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359831} :answer-field/id #uuid "4734d78b-ae84-5fd9-bd5c-81bd2a684821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10fb8ec2-9c2e-55ae-85b0-700b4fea54e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94631" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $H={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $H$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f4af805-3bdf-5fa9-90c0-ed985d6b0743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358583} {:answer/id #uuid "61d918ba-bcc1-58e6-98c3-e55cd2976c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358603} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8645ba-8ed7-55f7-bb66-4172562bd279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359144} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b4978e-740e-5afa-a333-67c98a82249a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359193} {:answer/id #uuid "d87fb52c-1b84-5ee5-8a3f-db59967a3489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d918ba-bcc1-58e6-98c3-e55cd2976c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358603} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7663cb0-dd8e-5af0-842c-4853b4c5638f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6847dae1-289b-5050-b4ae-95ca49b57e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-94632" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under function addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & =x+(1-x) \\\\  & =(x-x)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot x=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2352dc01-d78b-5e4d-9ea2-e3713f96c232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358062} {:answer/id #uuid "4912a4af-50d5-51b2-898d-9ff48ad0aac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358380} {:answer/id #uuid "889055ff-bdf2-5664-ace5-f571cb298cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358947} {:answer/id #uuid "90b40ae1-7406-5ce7-aca6-21dd75553b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359018} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0fe1f3-9769-5272-94fa-c21402464bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "889055ff-bdf2-5664-ace5-f571cb298cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8b075c5-1dd5-565f-a18b-3f5b7c1118ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ee30622-b701-5da4-9a44-4e5ce669fd87" :question/math-academy-id "q-94633" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $Q$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Q$ is closed under function addition\n- $Q$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Q$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $Q$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $Q$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35bf1f92-d99f-5f30-83ab-72b00ca6e41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358230} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1f66e3-1e1f-531d-b3ba-be591d849917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358395} {:answer/id #uuid "4c54e437-70cc-54dc-8eba-897cd404c4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358411} {:answer/id #uuid "56bcf1bd-1ce7-50d2-af4d-023ac7d55d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358505} {:answer/id #uuid "92cf59b6-875b-524c-b55a-4dcb1b4f7a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92cf59b6-875b-524c-b55a-4dcb1b4f7a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359037} :answer-field/id #uuid "f460ac3f-14d2-5bd6-af56-97e945372382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f62d508-5cf6-5993-b139-949d11db546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94635" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $C$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under function addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=1-{x}^{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & ={x}^{3}+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-{x}^{3})+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $C$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64363b46-80c6-5a50-ab9c-746262226df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358630} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce1da6e-66b8-5479-9d58-51a806de7840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358705} {:answer/id #uuid "e11342c0-bb02-5f90-a9fa-0550740458cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359738} {:answer/id #uuid "e43c60e4-d331-5ee7-ace6-79d73b2d76c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359777} {:answer/id #uuid "e6475d02-1b51-5692-bdfa-d5fcfdbcf944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce1da6e-66b8-5479-9d58-51a806de7840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358705} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bd9399b-f38b-5f31-90a5-e5e6508b92dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44096d11-ac2c-5302-8668-0431e5543e9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94642" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-2,2]\\to R$ and its subset $E$ containing all even functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $E$ is closed under function addition\n- $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $E$ is a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n- $E$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in E,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(-x) & =f(-x)+g(-x) \\\\  & =f(x)+g(x) \\\\  & =(f+g)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in E$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(-x) & =kf(-x) \\\\  & =kf(x) \\\\  & =(kf)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ec2112-fa0f-5d22-b4dc-660556a2e1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358040} {:answer/id #uuid "6f83a5db-5ddb-530e-8a46-35a668b30654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358721} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b8447b-5242-51b4-8cd0-6a93d8129148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359298} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed6a28-47fe-52dc-9756-443743ba4903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359331} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6bfc10-bd89-5c96-b13c-8f7492823f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f83a5db-5ddb-530e-8a46-35a668b30654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358721} :answer-field/id #uuid "125ae5b4-a75a-5792-b96b-71cebaf2b26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ff3e8b0-9d12-58ec-aef1-61a782559716" :question/math-academy-id "q-94649" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46cbdbdd-61b8-51d3-907f-c4d506b9d061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358360} {:answer/id #uuid "52f90faf-a0d8-517f-bed0-d8b555e33493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358465} {:answer/id #uuid "8466d8df-d800-5edb-b78d-c91b13b6e449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358915} {:answer/id #uuid "8f74af51-4b88-590d-93f2-6606c292b107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359001} {:answer/id #uuid "cef60181-7c24-5b5b-b23c-0a7ab830b370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f90faf-a0d8-517f-bed0-d8b555e33493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358465} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f6bb20-e87d-5e8c-86a3-f4afdfd0d828" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998609c4-bfb7-5e7d-ad89-b03b40316ca2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94661" :question/problem "Let $F$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $F.$\n- For the zero polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F$ such that $p(x)+q(x)\\ne q(x)+p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot x+0.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F,$ we have that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of polynomials is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53851ace-d831-5af3-8b5b-578067fa8481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358474} {:answer/id #uuid "5da7d8e2-4a13-5a9c-9bb3-df4c0f338b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358570} {:answer/id #uuid "6b55ac55-a0f9-5b3c-bf8c-189dc1c5c087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358692} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e56af9-fbb6-5fb0-8ef1-075aa37c6efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359207} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6118a0-42da-5983-b2c3-c9d4acea2652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e56af9-fbb6-5fb0-8ef1-075aa37c6efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359207} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e04fd26-f441-5ec5-92a1-b030caca41e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86d246c9-ffba-5c6c-b83e-58058d87e01e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94686" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2}+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$\n- For the constant polynomial $1$ and any $p(x)\\in E,$ we have $1+p(x)=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in E$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the constant polynomial $1$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n1=0\\cdot {x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nHence, $1\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n1+p(x)=1+{x}^{2}+1={x}^{2}+2\\ne p(x).\n$$\n\nThe zero-polynomial $0$ is the unique polynomial such that for any $p(x)\\in E$ we have $0+p(x)=p(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in E,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e019c0c-c668-5ed1-a867-43f40e187002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358011} {:answer/id #uuid "93241e3f-6a8f-5ad8-bfac-2641dcb5f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359039} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1ff7cd-a746-57e7-b6af-1dcad05500df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359224} {:answer/id #uuid "ba745c6e-4d9f-526e-b1ed-68947cdeb6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359373} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fa2cf0-d079-54dc-a60a-17daf11e5c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e019c0c-c668-5ed1-a867-43f40e187002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358011} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fea600c-8657-5d8b-912c-ccccb2894d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d30a6a74-1882-58de-a0e8-caf74485928f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94700" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in R,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in R,$ we have $-1(-1\\cdot A)=-A.$\n- For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij}\n$$\n\nand, hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$  Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1(-1\\cdot A) & =-1(-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1([\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\ne -A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7101e1-7d10-5b45-abb3-fa5608aa1612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357870} {:answer/id #uuid "15912af8-6c4b-59e2-ba54-bc8c9234e263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357941} {:answer/id #uuid "262f801c-3ae8-51d4-ace5-5c12b77eec0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358097} {:answer/id #uuid "7bba31db-5a90-53fc-bf2b-a7a3e9503ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358825} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c9c3c0-5615-50d2-b23c-3a96c9d7e8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262f801c-3ae8-51d4-ace5-5c12b77eec0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358097} :answer-field/id #uuid "870102b1-a0c7-5195-ac87-d2cf8f11e47a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0778fc80-bc7a-5da8-b455-9670cb2d5afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94711" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))=2p(x)-2q(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f742921-44c0-5555-91e3-ff3f6b2d73b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358867} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca67e1d-0710-54c7-a1c9-f23341332558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358974} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe88c59-8189-5430-a2ac-afabd28b067b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359005} {:answer/id #uuid "d155cd63-1a1f-53fc-98b6-16a73f6e20e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359583} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b44ada-7134-53ec-a1af-330211568ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1b44ada-7134-53ec-a1af-330211568ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359585} :answer-field/id #uuid "a89a103d-a18e-5d9c-96d3-aed67b8f2b77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03514ad7-d8b6-5b82-8d41-07ae43d6027c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94723" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2x\\in T.$\n- The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e9ffc2c-f7e9-51f5-ba89-e08fb8f3432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358994} {:answer/id #uuid "be01d135-70e6-5207-99f2-9da737bd038e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359408} {:answer/id #uuid "cb65c933-0ae4-5e0b-ad3a-d5ac3265df55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359539} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0974db-219c-5c9a-91e4-4eb32811b299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359565} {:answer/id #uuid "dea888bd-01f3-5ef6-97fd-015ad1524c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ffc2c-f7e9-51f5-ba89-e08fb8f3432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358994} :answer-field/id #uuid "c098feba-a034-5912-a2d6-6f3e33aec00e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96ce050e-6292-5b2a-b7c7-0c1f7f51192d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94731" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-1)^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1-9 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0315cd7d-1301-5b67-a549-7cd0a5e92892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357785} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc1f5e7-b89a-5b03-a3a0-4ebb7eb1e543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358000} {:answer/id #uuid "97b1f7db-3346-53a7-b0aa-9bb2c8853533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359080} {:answer/id #uuid "e01cae1e-f0d7-5a0a-a7ba-1d2522aa07ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359720} {:answer/id #uuid "fafd1fa4-cb58-5f3d-ae7d-cb6d37785672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc1f5e7-b89a-5b03-a3a0-4ebb7eb1e543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358000} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f652820-724c-504a-923b-f8719a50d22b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c28bdf3-28d7-5fd9-bd3d-116dde5b3b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-94734" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{2}^{2}+7{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-6)^{2}+7\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =36+0 \\\\  & =36>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-72<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={0}^{2}-(-6)^{2}-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-36-0 \\\\  & =-36<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{: